  <keyword>ode tolerances tutorial</keyword>
  <short_description>Control ODE accuracy and statistics.</short_description>
  <description>
    <p>Use <b>RelTol</b> for relative accuracy and <b>AbsTol</b> for small solution components. Use <b>InitialStep</b>, <b>MaxStep</b>, and <b>MinStep</b> to bound the adaptive step controller.</p>
    <table style="width:100%">
  <tr><th>Option</th><th>Effect</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>RelTol</b></td><td>Scales the local error test with the solution size.</td><td>Primary accuracy control.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>AbsTol</b></td><td>Sets a floor for small solution components.</td><td>Protects variables near zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>NormControl</b></td><td>Uses a vector norm in the adaptive error test.</td><td>Coupled systems with shared scale.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Stats</b></td><td>Reports solver counters.</td><td>Diagnostics and regression tests.</td></tr>
</table>
    <p>Set <b>Stats</b> to <b>on</b> to display integration counts, or read the <b>stats</b> field from a solution structure.</p>
  </description>
  <examples>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description>Compare two tolerances.</example_item_description>
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[loose = odeset('RelTol', 1e-3, 'AbsTol', 1e-6);
tight = odeset('RelTol', 1e-6, 'AbsTol', 1e-9);
solLoose = ode45(@(t,y) y, [0 1], 1, loose);
solTight = ode45(@(t,y) y, [0 1], 1, tight);
[solLoose.stats.nsteps solTight.stats.nsteps] ]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description>Display statistics.</example_item_description>
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[options = odeset('Stats', 'on', 'MaxStep', 0.1);
[t, y] = ode45(@(t,y) -y, [0 1], 1, options);]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
  </examples>
